# ping -- caps.decl
#
# Format: R90-XREPO.013.M1-001 line-oriented caps.decl grammar
# (paideia-os design/architecture/caps-decl-format.md).
#
# `!KIND_FOO <mask>` = mandatory: exec fails -EACCES if the parent's
# cap-set does not hold at least this mask. ` KIND_FOO <mask>` (no
# `!`) = optional: narrowed to at most this mask, dropped to zero if
# the parent held none.
#
# ping's role: minimal `ping <host>` DNS-resolve + ICMP-echo + RTT
# loop (paideia-os design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md). Distinct
# from the full-featured `paideia-os/pdxping`.
#
# Kind rationale:
#   KIND_USER          -- invoker attribution (audit record identity);
#                         every satellite in the R100 wave lists this.
#   KIND_TTY           -- write the four "reply from ..." lines to the
#                         invoker's terminal (fd 1). No read /
#                         tcsetattr needed.
#
# What is deliberately NOT declared here: no network-capability kind
# at all. Both of this tool's network-shaped operations are honest
# WEAK stubs at v0.5.0 (see README.md) -- DNS resolution never calls
# `libpdx-net.net_resolve` (not yet linkable) and ICMP echo never
# calls the kernel's real `sys_icmp_echo` (SC+ ID 103), because that
# syscall's `R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL` gate
# (src/kernel/core/cap/cap_net_privileged.pdx) admits only boot
# context and PID 1 at this wave -- an ordinary ring-3 `ping` process
# would get `-EPERM` on every real call regardless of any cap this
# file could declare (the gate checks PID, not a capability slot).
# When a real resolver and a real per-task ICMP grant exist, this file
# grows the corresponding `KIND_*` entries; declaring them now would
# claim access this tool cannot actually exercise, which the R100
# wave's "declared for shape, not touched" convention still requires
# to be truthful about what the code paths actually do.
!KIND_USER               0x001   # invoker attribution (audit)
!KIND_TTY                0x002   # write-only: reply lines to stdout
